WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
Christian Elder, Screenwriter: Being&nbsp;
a writer of putting in those hours of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:13.680
spending that time of building your voice as&nbsp;
a writer and to have conviction as a writer.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.720
Film Courage: I believe you've referenced&nbsp;
Dorothy Parker many times where you say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.480
the famous remark of her’s “I hate&nbsp;
to write, I love having written.” We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.980
were talking a little bit about her off&nbsp;
screen how do you relate to that quote.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:38.580
Christian: Absolutely I relate&nbsp;
to that quote. Writing is hard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:49.500
It's tough for a couple of reasons. There's two&nbsp;
sides of that coin that the actual activity itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.760 --> 00:01:00.360
is is taxing and it requires a lot of&nbsp;
patience and also it is one of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:08.760
chosen vocations where one is likely to suffer&nbsp;
a great deal of critique, scrutiny and attack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:20.880
All of that together makes it makes it tough&nbsp;
so you have to sort of build a sort of thick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:33.420
skin. Now with that comes the great myths&nbsp;
of the writers and and all the writers that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:45.120
I can think of and this is across all genres of&nbsp;
writing most of them have this aura of strength&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:54.780
and promotion of that strength as a writer&nbsp;
and in their writing and those and the finest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.780 --> 00:02:07.140
examples of it is Dorothy Parker is a probably&nbsp;
one of the pinnacles of that idea of strength&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:15.780
as well as someone of course like Ernest&nbsp;
Hemingway, Hunter S. Thompson, James Baldwin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:31.020
all of these characters convey strength and that&nbsp;
is a quality that comes from having to deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:40.740
the rigors of being a writer of putting in those&nbsp;
hours of spending that time of building your voice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:50.580
as a writer and to have conviction as a writer and&nbsp;
and it also speaks to something a little bit that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:56.160
we talked about just a few minutes ago which was&nbsp;
that the character that you were referring to in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:01.440
Michael Clayton and that is they don't really&nbsp;
fit in this box they don't fit in that box&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:10.620
there's not really a place for them but they can't&nbsp;
just fade away this is true, this is absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:15.780
true I think that's an interesting analogy and&nbsp;
an interesting point that you're bringing up

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:36.900
I think that once you go down the road as is it&nbsp;
I always get I'm Thoreau and Emerson I'm confused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:47.700
but I believe it was Thoreau The Road Not Taken&nbsp;
so to speak once you go down the road not taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:57.300
a friend of mine used to say it's it gets a little&nbsp;
sketchy down there because the road not taken is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:09.540
often patrolled by the cops so once you go down&nbsp;
that road don't expect to not face adversity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:23.760
and don't expect it to not change you don't&nbsp;
expect to not to not make you something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:32.220
you don't expect that you're going to be you&nbsp;
will you will more than likely become smarter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:42.000
wiser you will you will have survival skills that&nbsp;
you probably didn't expect that you would gain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:52.620
but it will turn you into something else that's&nbsp;
really interesting for better or worse yes you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.620 --> 00:05:00.780
right I like what you said how going down this&nbsp;
road that there's not it's kind of a dark murky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:05.940
road where you're not really sure what's in the&nbsp;
shadows but it also is maybe sort of a metaphor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:13.200
for what a lot of writers have been maybe more&nbsp;
alone or I know outcast is is not the right word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:21.600
but they're used to having to be their own&nbsp;
strength well I mean to a degree I mean like

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:33.060
I know a lot of writers and I've spent a lot&nbsp;
of time around writers this is a whole other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:48.360
subject matter we can get into this in a bit&nbsp;
but but being alone as a writer is not so bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.800 --> 00:06:00.780
because writers aren't really alone it's&nbsp;
a kind of illusion because writers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:10.020
besieged with the often with especially if they're&nbsp;
writing stories they're besieged with characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:21.900
that live with them so and it's this misnomer to&nbsp;
say a writer is alone in fact it's like writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:29.340
writers in a sense if you want to if you want&nbsp;
to call them anti-social they aren't anti-social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:37.020
by I think by nature they're anti-social by choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:42.220
because they just want to get back&nbsp;
to the people they know [Laughter]

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:56.040
they're you're currently entertained&nbsp;
with and I think that's really what it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:07.140
I think also conversely many writers&nbsp;
are extraordinarily gregarious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:16.620
my personal M.O. [modus operandi]&nbsp;
I think is that I have two sides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:26.100
and those two sides are I can be&nbsp;
incredibly quiet totally bashful really shy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:34.080
a real introvert and then I swing way to&nbsp;
the other side and I can be incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:42.300
gregarious and extrovert and and highly&nbsp;
social and and loud and boisterous and all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:51.180
those things I don't know what that means&nbsp;
and but I do know that that it that is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:59.460
that is not something that is unusual that&nbsp;
that many, many writers are fill that mold.

